mins\n\nDining\n\n###### Meet the fourth generation heir of Tong Heng, the egg tart empire founded in the 1920s in Singapore\n\nStyle & Beauty\n\n###### Do you know the symbolic meaning behind the Indian sari? Plus the best places to shop\n\nDining\n\n###### Tradition is their cup of tea: What's the story behind Pek Sin Choon in Chinatown?--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKreta Ayer Heritage Gallery (https://www.roots.gov.sg/stories-landing/stories/Kreta-Ayer-Heritage-Gallery/)\nciteturn5search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.1 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Through photographs, stories and objects from the community, this gallery showcases the rich cultural heritage of the Chinese community in Chinatown. ... Kreta Ayer Heritage Gallery – Introduction Chinese Opera Elements of Chinese Opera Local Dialect Forms of Chinese Opera Memories of Chinese Opera Chinese Puppet Theatre Nanyin, Music of the South Chinese Calligraphy Traditional Chinese Teahouse and Tea Appreciation Living Traditions Explore 3D Space ... In fact, Chinese opera became so popular here during its heyday that Singapore was known as the “second homeland of Cantonese opera” outside of Hong Kong and China!\n\n# Kreta Ayer Heritage Gallery\n\n### \n\n###### \n\nImage: img-fluid\n\nIntroduction\n\nOpening Hours\n\nTuesday – Sunday\n\n10.00AM – 6.00PM\n\n \n\nNotice on Regular Opening Hours\n\nPlease be informed that the Gallery is closed to the public on weekdays during the school term from 9.00AM - 4.30PM.\n\n \n\nIf you would like to arrange for a visit during these periods, please send your inquiry to: nhb_kahg_mbl@nhb.gov.sg. Visits without prior appointment during the above-mentioned times will be restricted.\n\n* * *\n\n#### Kreta Ayer Heritage Gallery\n\nKreta Ayer Heritage Gallery provides an overview of the history and heritage of Kreta Ayer, and highlights the area’s rich and diverse intangible cultural heritage. These include cultural art forms such as Chinese opera, Chinese puppetry, nanyin music, tea appreciation and calligraphy.\n\nThrough photographs, stories and objects from the community, this gallery showcases the rich cultural heritage of the Chinese community in Chinatown. Step into the Kreta Ayer Heritage Gallery to find out more about the above-mentioned cultural art forms and trace their evolution from the days of old Chinatown to contemporary times.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLOCAL CULTURAL ACTIVITIES - Mercure ICON Singapore City Centre (https://www.mercure-icon-singapore-city-centre.com/local-cultural-activities/)\nciteturn5search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Discover the art of Chinese tea appreciation at Tea Chapter, a charming heritage teahouse in the heart of Chinatown.\n\n# LOCAL CULTURAL ACTIVITIES\n\nImmerse yourself in the rich flavours and traditions of Singapore, where cultural experiences come alive through time-honoured practices and local delicacies.\n\nCHINESE TEA APPRECIATION EXPERIENCE\n\nDiscover the art of Chinese tea appreciation at Tea Chapter, a charming heritage teahouse in the heart of Chinatown. Begin with an introduction to the traditional tea set and its purpose, followed by a graceful demonstration of time-honoured brewing techniques. Savour the experience with Aged Narcissus, a roasted oolong tea, paired with soft, fluffy steamed buns filled with the well-known chilli crab-inspired filling.\n\nEnjoy 10% off on retail items when you present your keycard.\n\nDuration: 30 – 40 mins\nPrice: SGD 18 per person (subject to prevailing government tax)\nOpening Hours: 1 1am to 9pm daily\nAddress: 9 Neil Road, Singapore 088808 (7-min walk from hotel)\n\nPlease call +65 6226 1175 (1 1am to 9pm) or email inquiry@teachapter.com at least one day in advance to book your experience. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSiam Express Pte Ltd (https://www.siamexpress.com.sg/tour/Tea%20ceremony%20experience%20%40%20Local%20Heritage%20Teahouse/17)\nciteturn5search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;   * Join us for a tea ceremony at Tea Chapter, housed within a heritage building in Chinatown. \n  * Learn about the background and culture of Chinese tea ceremonies and relish in the calm of the settings; Tea Chapter is located in Singapore's first restored conservation shophouse, where the late Queen Elizabeth II visited during her time in Singapore.\n\n# Tea ceremony experience @ Local Heritage Teahouse\n\n###  \n\nOVERVIEW\n\nWHAT TO EXPECT\n\nOPTIONS\n\nREMARKS\n\n $65/pax (Adult & Child same price)\n\n  * Join us for a tea ceremony at Tea Chapter, housed within a heritage building in Chinatown. \n  * Learn about the background and culture of Chinese tea ceremonies and relish in the calm of the settings; Tea Chapter is located in Singapore's first restored conservation shophouse, where the late Queen Elizabeth II visited during her time in Singapore.\n  * Relax over a hot cup of tea and light refreshments, including braised tea egg and tea cookies.\n      * Tea Ceremony & Sharing Session on Health Benefits of tea\n    * Tea / Refreshment Menu:\n    * > 1 Pot of Imperial Golden Cassia Tea Per Table\n    * > 1 Braised Tea Egg or Cookie Per Pax\n\nDaily - 2 Slots :  11 am &  2 pm\n\n\nSIAM EXPRESS PTE LTD\n\nTA00424\n(65) 6733 0077\naccts@siamexpress.com.sg\n\nSitemap\n\nOTHER CONTACTS:\n\nFax: (65) 6737 1435\nEmergency Contact: (65) 9069 0683\n\nOne Commonwealth\n1 Commonwealth Lane #07-03\nSingapore 149544\n\nWhy Choose Us:\n\n  * Excellent track record\n  * Competitive rates\n  * Dedicated team for FITs, Groups and Events / Incentives\n  * Highly motivated and experienced staff\n  * One-stop shop travel-related service provider--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Perfect Brew: Singapore’s Chinese Tea Culture | BiblioAsia (https://biblioasia.nlb.gov.sg/all-sections/vol-22-issue-2-jul-sep-2026-the-perfect-brew--singapore-s-chinese-tea-culture/)\nciteturn5search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 6 days ago; Singapore’s Chinese tea heritage tells a story where traditional practices, community and adaptive innovation have shaped an evolving yet enduring cultural legacy. ... From herbal milk teas at Amacha (阿嬷茶语) in Chinatown to tea-inspired cocktails at the modern restaurant-bar Synthesis, novelty teas are embraced by Gen Z and millennials, who often feature them on social media. ... From the 1980s onwards, Chinese tea art, or zhonghua chayi (中华茶艺), became popular in China and Taiwan. ... ^{9} These teahouses often occupy conserved shophouses or intimate upper-storey spaces, with specially curated tea rooms for retreat and quiet conversation.\n\n# The Perfect Brew: Singapore’s Chinese Tea Culture\n\n18 May 2026\n\nSingapore’s Chinese tea heritage tells a story where traditional practices, community and adaptive innovation have shaped an evolving yet enduring cultural legacy.\n\nBy Ng Yun Ling\n\nImage: Teawares and tea accessories at Tea Chapter, 2026. Photo by Ng Yun Ling.\n\nTeawares and tea accessories at Tea Chapter, 2026. Photo by Ng Yun Ling.\n\nToday, if you come across a long and winding queue in a shopping mall in Singapore, it is likely for the latest bubble tea outlet or modern tea beverage chain to hit our shores. From herbal milk teas at Amacha (阿嬷茶语) in Chinatown to tea-inspired cocktails at the modern restaurant-bar Synthesis, novelty teas are embraced by Gen Z and millennials, who often feature them on social media.\n\nBut before bubble tea arrived here in 1992, drinking Chinese tea meant visiting an artisanal teahouse to experience the fine art of tea brewing. Here, one could sip tea from dainty teacups, listen to live Chinese classical music from a guzheng or pipa, admire paintings on the wall and even indulge in Chinese calligraphy – all in a cosy and relaxing ambience to meet up with friends over a tête-à-tête.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTender for Mid Autumn Festive Fair | People's Association (https://www.pa.gov.sg/tender-details/tender-for-mid-autumn-festive-fair/)\nciteturn5search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; Interested tenderers are invited to tender for the Chinatown Mid-Autumn Fair as follows: ... Closing Date & Time: 19 May 2025, 06:00 PM\n\n# Tender for Mid Autumn Festive Fair\n\nLearn more about the tender details and documents\n\nLast updated on 20 January 2026\n\n## Tender for Mid-Autumn Festive Fair\n\n## Details\n\nInterested tenderers are invited to tender for the Chinatown Mid-Autumn Fair as follows:\n\nVenue: Open Space in front of People’s Park Complex\n\nFair Duration: 7 September 2025 to 6 October 2025 (30 days)\n\nCalling Committee: Chinatown Festival Committee\n\nPublication Date: 2 May 2025\n\nClosing Date & Time: 19 May 2025, 06:00 PM\n\n## Tender Document\n\nInterested Tenderers may request for a copy of tender specifications by emailing to Lee_Xiaohui@pa.gov.sg and Chng_Pei_Joo@pa.gov.sg.\n\n## For Enquiry\n\nPlease contact Lee Xiao Hui or Chng Pei Joo at 6222 3597 or email to Lee_Xiaohui@pa.gov.sgand Chng_Pei_Joo@pa.gov.sg.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTracing Chinese Roots & Tasting Tea | Destination Asia (https://www.destination-asia.com/itineraries/tracing-chinese-roots-tasting-tea/)\nciteturn5search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago;   * Explore the Chinatown Heritage Centre, featuring restored shophouses and immersive exhibits on early Chinese immigrant life. ...   * Brew and taste traditional Chinese tea while understanding its cultural significance and mindful preparation techniques.\n\n# Tracing Chinese Roots & Tasting Tea\n\n  * Singapore\n  * 4 Hours\n  * Arts & Culture\n\n## What to expect\n\nWithin the historic streets of Chinatown, this half-day visit traces the experiences of Singapore’s early Chinese community. At the Chinatown Heritage Centre, set within restored twentieth-century shophouses, carefully recreated interiors and exhibits illustrate the lives of early settlers and the development of the district as a centre of trade and residence. The setting offers a grounded perspective on the challenges and resilience that shaped this neighbourhood.\n\nThe experience continues at YiXing Xuan Teahouse, where the practice of traditional Chinese tea preparation is introduced. Guided by experienced hosts, the session explores the principles behind tea culture, including the selection of leaves and methods of brewing. Tasting a variety of teas provides a direct connection to this long-established tradition, reflecting its focus on balance, ritual and mindfulness, and offering a considered close to the visit.\n\n## Highlights\n\n  * Explore the Chinatown Heritage Centre, featuring restored shophouses and immersive exhibits on early Chinese immigrant life.\n  * Discover recreated 1950s interiors, offering a rare glimpse into the living conditions of Chinatown’s early communities.\n  * Learn about different tea varieties and how to recognise their characteristics during a guided tea appreciation session.\n  * Brew and taste traditional Chinese tea while understanding its cultural significance and mindful preparation techniques.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAmacha 阿嬷茶语 - Chinatown Heritage Centre - 46 Pagoda St, Singapore | Corner (https://www.corner.inc/place/p28BgW2CSLhK)\nciteturn5search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Herbal tea shop inside the Chinatown Heritage Centre doing boba-format drinks built around traditional Chinese medicinal ingredients.\n\n# Amacha 阿嬷茶语 - Chinatown Heritage Centre\n\n🌿️herbal milk tea\n\n46 Pagoda St, Singapore • Chinatown\n\n15 SAVES\n\nGrandma's remedy cabinet reimagined as a milk tea bar\n\nclosed\n\nWednesday: 10:30 AM – 9:30 PM\n\nUpdated August 18th, 2026\n\nDirectionsSiteCall\n\nThe herbal blends here are genuinely restorative - think chrysanthemum milk tea and barley-pandan-ginger rather than anything overly sweet or trendy. Rustic-clean interior with a handful of seats inside and more out front, perfect for a slow afternoon pause between sights.\n\n### VIBE\n\nHerbal tea shop inside the Chinatown Heritage Centre doing boba-format drinks built around traditional Chinese medicinal ingredients. Chrysanthemum, barley, dried orange peel, monk fruit - the kind of stuff your grandma actually used.\n\n### WHAT TO GET\n\nGrandma's Remedy (chrysanthemum milk tea with herbal grass jelly) at 0% sugar so the tea flavor actually comes through. The Damp Drainer if you want something lighter with pandan and ginger running through the barley base.\n\n### THE MOVE\n\nOrder via QR code to skip the queue. Cashless payment only, no cash accepted.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStreet Corner Heritage Galleries: Chinatown (https://www.roots.gov.sg/stories-landing/stories/Street-Corner-Heritage-Galleries-Chinatown)\nciteturn5search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.1 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; By the 1910s, the family’s traditional Chinese medicine business, rebranded as “Eu Yan Sang”, had extended to Singapore, Hong Kong, and southern China. ... Today, the restaurant is run by the third generation of the Ho family who remains committed to preserving local Chinese food heritage and offering a Singaporean interpretation of Cantonese cuisine to the younger generation. ... Teahouses like Tong Heng were a cornerstone of Cantonese culture in pre-war Chinatown where patrons would gather to drink tea, enjoy confectioneries and chat.\n\nImage: img-fluid\n\nThis collection of crockery was used by the restaurant during wedding banquets in the past, and the bowls and teacups are decorated with dragons, which are considered an auspicious symbol signifying vitality and strength.\n\n#### From Traditional Teahouse to Delicious Pastries: Tong Heng\n\nImage\n\nRecognised for its diamond-shaped egg tarts today, Tong Heng has its roots as a pre-war establishment known as Huang Hou Teahouse.\n\nIts founder, Fong Chee Heng, who arrived in the 1910s from Guangdong, China, started out as an itinerant hawker peddling drinks in various teahouses. With savings accumulated over the years, Fong bought over the teahouse on Smith Street in 1934, which was later renamed “Tong Heng”.\n\nTeahouses like Tong Heng were a cornerstone of Cantonese culture in pre-war Chinatown where patrons would gather to drink tea, enjoy confectioneries and chat. Located across opera theatre Lai Chun Yuen, Tong Heng was also where fans would purchase sweet treats for their favourite stars.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBIBLIOASIA\nJUL–SEP 2026\nFEATURE\nISSUE 02\nVOL. 22\nN (https://isomer-user-content.by.gov.sg/339/ab240cdf-7d22-4002-a825-fd0e509954d4/BA-Jul-Sep2026_FA_TeaCulture.pdf)\nciteturn5search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; channelnewsasia.com/dining/tong-heng-egg-tart-chinatown-singapore-386311. ... “From Old Cemetery to Vibrant New Town; A Peck San Theng Heritage Gallery,” ... Singaporeans Turn Chinese Teapot Collectors,” Straits Times, 20 February 1994, ... aroma of tea and the sound of singing], Xin Ming Ri Bao 新明日报, 6 April 1994,\n3\t\nJoyce Yang, “Meet the Fourth Generation Heir of Tong Heng, the Egg Tart Empire \nFounded in the 1920s in Singapore,” CNA, 9 March 2024, https://cnalifestyle.\nchannelnewsasia.com/dining/tong-heng-egg-tart-chinatown-singapore-386311.\n4\t\nHan Shanyuan 韩山元, “Bu hui xiaoshi de gulao hangye guangzhou fengwei de \nxinjiapo chalou” 不会消失的古老行业 广州风味的新加坡茶楼 [An ancient \nindustry that will never disappear: Guangzhou-style Singapore teahouse], \nLianhe Wanbao 联合晚报, 24 June 1983, 3. (From NewspaperSG)\n5\t\nWood 木, “Guangdong de ‘yin cha’” 广东的 ‘饮茶’ [“Drinking tea” in Guangdong], \nLianhe Wanbao 联合晚报, 18 M